1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"library--comprehensible-input","kind":"library-article","route":"/roadmap/library/comprehensible-input","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/library/comprehensible-input","sourceHash":"2ddb84216a78e52ae89b80d645dfa1f60b6784736610418882958f543b6fd8c3","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T16:52:42.065Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"Comprehensible Input","zh":"可理解输入"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"segment-0001","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Comprehensible input is target-language content that you can mostly understand. The idea, rooted in Stephen Krashen's Input Hypothesis, is that language acquisition happens when you're exposed to input that's just slightly above your current level — enough to challenge you, but not so much that you're lost.","zh":"可理解输入是你基本能够理解的目标语言内容。这个理念源自 Stephen Krashen 的输入假说：当你接触到只比当前水平略高的输入时，语言习得就会发生——既足以带来挑战，又不会难到让你完全摸不着头绪。"}},{"id":"why-it-matters","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Why It Matters","zh":"为什么它很重要"}},{"id":"segment-0003","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Your brain can't acquire language from input it doesn't understand at all. If you listen to a language you've never studied, nothing sticks no matter how many hours you listen. But if you understand 70-90% of what you hear, your brain can use context (or tools) to figure out the remaining 10-30% — and that's where acquisition happens.","zh":"你的大脑无法从完全不理解的输入中习得语言。如果你听一种从未学过的语言，无论听多少小时都留不下什么印象。但如果你能理解所听内容的 70%～90%，大脑就可以利用语境（或工具）弄懂剩余的 10%～30%——而习得正是在这里发生的。"}},{"id":"segment-0004","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Getting from understanding nothing at all to that 70-90% is what the first few Phases are all about. Learning words, going slowly and building up your ability to understand.","zh":"最初几个阶段的核心，就是让你从完全听不懂进步到能够理解 70%～90%。学习词汇，放慢脚步，逐步培养理解能力。"}},{"id":"finding-comprehensible-input","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Finding Comprehensible Input","zh":"寻找可理解输入"}},{"id":"segment-0006","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"There is a slight difference between \"comprehensible input\" and \"Comprehensible Input.\" The first one refers to any content that is comprehensible. The second one is a specific genre of language learning content that's made for people who are new to the language, in order to be comprehensible.","zh":"“可理解的输入”和“可理解输入”之间存在细微差别。前者泛指任何能够理解的内容；后者则是一种专为语言初学者制作、以确保内容容易理解为目的的特定语言学习内容类型。"}},{"id":"segment-0007","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Foundations (Phase 1):</strong> Content made specifically for learners — YouTube channels, apps, graded podcasts. Some channels create Comprehensible Input (the genre) content using slow speech, visual aids, and limited vocabulary.","zh":"<strong>基础期（阶段 1）：</strong>专门为学习者制作的内容——YouTube 频道、应用程序、分级播客。有些频道会利用慢速言语、视觉辅助和有限词汇，制作“可理解输入”这一类型的内容。"}},{"id":"segment-0008","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Beginner (Phase 1-2):</strong> Content made for natives, but that's simpler or easier — Kids TV shows, basic vlogs or even dubs/translations of content you've consumed before. This kind of material is especially important for languages without much specially made Comprehensible Input.","zh":"<strong>初级（阶段 1～2）：</strong>为母语者制作、但相对简单或容易的内容——儿童电视节目、基础视频博客，甚至是你以前看过的内容的配音版或翻译版。对于缺少专门制作的可理解输入内容的语言，这类材料尤其重要。"}},{"id":"segment-0009","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Intermediate (Phase 2):</strong> Native content made comprehensible through tools — TV shows with subtitles, dictionaries, pausing. The content isn't simplified, but your tools make it comprehensible.","zh":"<strong>中级（阶段 2）：</strong>借助工具变得可理解的母语内容——带字幕观看电视剧、使用词典、暂停播放。内容本身没有经过简化，但你的工具让它变得可以理解。"}},{"id":"segment-0010","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Advanced (Phase 3+):</strong> Most native content becomes comprehensible without special tools. Your focus shifts from finding whatever comprehensible content you can to sifting through the hundreds or thousands of options to find what you're most interested in.","zh":"<strong>高级（阶段 3 及以后）：</strong>无需特殊工具，大多数母语内容也会变得可以理解。你的重点会从竭力寻找任何能理解的内容，转向从成百上千个选项中筛选出你最感兴趣的内容。"}},{"id":"tips","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Tips","zh":"建议"}},{"id":"segment-0012","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"Comprehensible input is a spectrum, not a binary. Some content is barely comprehensible, some is easily comprehensible. Both are valuable in different ways.","zh":"可理解输入是一个连续范围，并非非此即彼。有些内容勉强能理解，有些则很容易理解；两者各有不同的价值。"},{"en":"What counts as comprehensible for you will change as you improve! After you've learned for several hundred hours, you will be able to do much more than when you started.","zh":"随着你的进步，对你而言什么算“可理解”也会发生变化！学习几百个小时后，你能做到的事情将远多于刚开始时。"},{"en":"If you understand almost nothing, the content is too hard. Find something easier or add more support (subtitles, pre-reading, familiar content).","zh":"如果你几乎什么都听不懂，说明内容太难了。找更简单的内容，或增加更多辅助（字幕、预先阅读、熟悉的内容）。"},{"en":"If you understand everything effortlessly, the content might be too easy for maximum learning (but it's still great for freeflow practice).","zh":"如果你毫不费力就能理解全部内容，那么为了最大化学习效果，这些内容或许太简单了（但仍然很适合自由流畅练习）。"}]},{"id":"research-and-reasoning","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Research and Reasoning","zh":"研究与依据"}},{"id":"segment-0014","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The Input Hypothesis, formalized by Krashen (1982), is foundational to modern language acquisition theory. His central claim — that learners acquire language by understanding input that is slightly above their current level — has become a cornerstone of SLA research despite decades of debate about its exact scope and interaction with other factors.","zh":"Krashen（1982）正式提出的输入假说，是现代语言习得理论的基础。他的核心主张是：学习者通过理解略高于其当前水平的输入来习得语言。尽管几十年来，人们一直在讨论这一假说的确切适用范围及其与其他因素的相互作用，但它仍已成为第二语言习得研究的基石。"}},{"id":"segment-0015","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"The hypothesis predicts that input must be comprehensible for acquisition to occur. This is supported by research on attention and learning: learners must process meaning to establish the neural connections needed for language. Long (1996) expanded on Krashen's work with the Interaction Hypothesis, showing that it's not comprehensible input <em>per se</em> but comprehensible input combined with the opportunity to negotiate meaning that drives acquisition most effectively. We do that using language learning tools and interactive immersion.","zh":"该假说预测，输入必须可以理解，习得才会发生。注意力和学习方面的研究支持这一点：学习者必须处理意义，才能建立语言所需的神经连接。Long（1996）通过互动假说扩展了 Krashen 的研究，指出最有效地推动习得的，并不是可理解输入<em>本身</em>，而是可理解输入与协商意义的机会相结合。我们通过语言学习工具和互动式沉浸来做到这一点。"}}],"cards":[]}}]
